A FILTERED TRANSCLUSION CARRIES FIVE OPTIONAL PARTS, and the host binds two of them under names no
reader would guess: the run after the closing braces becomes `style`, and the dotted run after that
becomes `itemClass`.

A tooltip: {{{ [tag[Done]] |a tooltip}}} here.

A suffix, which binds as the style: {{{ [tag[Done]] }}suffix} here.

A class, which binds as the item class: {{{ [tag[Done]] }}}.my-class here.

Every part at once: {{{ [tag[Done]] |a tooltip||A/Template}}suffix}.my-class here.

A PIPE INSIDE THE FILTER ENDS THE FILTER — per the host's own transclusion-shorthand regex, which
splits on the first `|` wherever it falls, even inside a bracket operand: filteredtranscludeinline.js
builds `[tag[a` as its filter and `b]] ` as its tooltip below, never one filter operand. THE GRAMMAR
CANNOT PAINT THAT SPLIT: it reads the run through the shared filter rules, which know a `|` inside
`[tag[...]]` is operand text, not a separator — a divergence the shorthand's own regex owns alone,
not any other filter construct in this file:
{{{ [tag[a|b]] }}} here.

An empty filter builds nothing, and the braces read as text: {{{}}} here.
